WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.088
The second thing that changes
is the size of the job.

2
00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:06.112
Most migration plans
count users and groups.

3
00:00:06.112 --> 00:00:09.562
But Active Directory has also
been holding the estate together.

4
00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:15.849
DNS, DHCP and IP address management,
with the zones applications depend on.

5
00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:21.137
Certificate Services, behind smart
cards, Wi-Fi, VPN and servers.

6
00:00:21.137 --> 00:00:25.662
RADIUS through the Network Policy
Server for Wi-Fi and VPN sign-in.

7
00:00:25.662 --> 00:00:29.125
Applications that bind to
the directory over LDAP.

8
00:00:29.125 --> 00:00:32.625
The sync engine itself,
which is a tier zero asset.

9
00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:36.325
Devices that are domain-joined
and governed by Group Policy.

10
00:00:36.325 --> 00:00:39.988
Time, because Kerberos depends
on synchronized clocks.

11
00:00:39.988 --> 00:00:43.837
And file services, whose
permissions are bound to AD groups.

12
00:00:43.837 --> 00:00:47.262
None of those move just because
users sync to the cloud.

13
00:00:47.262 --> 00:00:51.798
Some, like the NPS extension for
Entra multifactor authentication,

14
00:00:51.798 --> 00:00:55.038
are useful interim steps
that still depend on AD.

15
00:00:55.038 --> 00:01:00.076
The program's answer is an adapter for
each dependency: an owner, a target, a

16
00:01:00.076 --> 00:01:02.595
cutover test and a validation check,

17
00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:06.100
retired in priority order
rather than all at once.
